const BANK: Array[Dictionary] = [
	# ---------- 1 The editor & engine basics ----------
	{"l":1,"q":"What does a game engine like Godot give you that you would otherwise have to build yourself?","o":["A reusable rendering, physics, input, and scene-management layer, so you write gameplay instead of plumbing","A single fixed template for every game, which you cannot change in any way","A library that only draws 2D sprites and does nothing else at all","A text editor with no runtime, so you still have to write your own renderer"],"w":"An engine is the shared infrastructure (drawing, physics, audio, input) that every game needs, so you spend your time on the game itself."},
	{"l":1,"q":"What is a \"node\" in Godot?","o":["The basic building block of a game: an object in the scene tree with its own behavior","A single pixel on the screen that the renderer draws one at a time","A text file that stores the project's settings","A network connection between two running instances of the game"],"w":"Everything in a running Godot game — sprites, cameras, UI, logic objects — is a node, or a script attached to one."},
	{"l":1,"q":"What is a \"scene\" (a .tscn file)?","o":["A saved tree of nodes that can be instanced (reused) wherever you need it","A single image file used as a texture","A compiled executable that runs without the Godot engine","A log file written while the game is running"],"w":"A scene packages a node and its children into a reusable unit, like a prefab."},
	{"l":1,"q":"In the running game, nodes are arranged in a \"scene tree.\" What does a node's position in that tree affect?","o":["Parent-child relationships: a child's transform, visibility, and process order are tied to its parent","Nothing: the tree is only shown in the editor and has no effect once the game runs","Only the node's name, never its behavior or appearance","Only how the project is saved to disk, not how it behaves while running"],"w":"Move a parent and its children move with it; hide a parent and its children are hidden too."},
	{"l":1,"q":"What's the difference between pressing F5 and F6 in the Godot editor?","o":["F5 runs the project's main scene; F6 runs whichever scene is currently open in the editor","F5 saves the project; F6 closes the project without saving","They do exactly the same thing, just with two different shortcuts","F5 runs the game on a phone; F6 runs it only on the desktop"],"w":"F6 (\"run current scene\") is handy for testing one scene in isolation without playing through the whole game to reach it."},
	{"l":1,"q":"What is the Inspector panel for?","o":["Viewing and editing the exported properties of the node currently selected in the Scene dock","Writing and editing GDScript source code","Browsing the folders and files in your project","Watching the frame rate and memory usage while the game runs"],"w":"Any @export variable on the selected node's script shows up here, ready to tweak without touching code."},
	{"l":1,"q":"What's the difference between the Scene dock and the FileSystem dock?","o":["Scene dock shows the node tree of the open scene; FileSystem dock shows your project's files and folders on disk","They are two names for the exact same panel, shown twice by mistake","Scene dock is for 3D projects only; FileSystem dock is for 2D projects only","FileSystem dock only appears after you export the project"],"w":"One is about the structure of the scene you're editing; the other is about the files that make up the whole project."},
	{"l":1,"q":"What happens when you \"instance\" a scene (for example, dragging a .tscn into another scene, or calling PackedScene.instantiate())?","o":["A new copy of that scene's node tree is created and can be added as a child elsewhere","The original scene file on disk is renamed","The game immediately starts running from that scene","Nothing happens until you restart the editor"],"w":"A scene is a template; instancing turns it into an actual node you can place, many times if you like."},

	# ---------- 2 Scene tree & nodes ----------
	{"l":2,"q":"If you move a parent Node2D, what happens to its children by default?","o":["They move with it, because a child's transform is relative to its parent's","They stay exactly where they were in world space, unaffected","They are automatically deleted","They swap places with the parent"],"w":"Local transforms are relative to the parent, so moving the parent carries every descendant along."},
	{"l":2,"q":"Node, Node2D, Node3D, and Control are all base classes you build on. What's the key difference between Node2D, Node3D, and Control?","o":["Node2D is for 2D games, Node3D is for 3D games, and Control is for building UI","They are four unrelated node types with nothing in common at all","Control can only exist inside a Node3D scene, never on its own","Node2D and Node3D are the exact same class under two different names"],"w":"Picking the right base class gives you the right built-in behavior: 2D transforms, 3D transforms, or UI layout."},
	{"l":2,"q":"What does $SomeChildName (or get_node(\"SomeChildName\")) do in a script?","o":["Looks up a child node by its path/name so you can call methods or read properties on it","Creates a brand new child node with that name","Deletes the child node with that name","Renames the current node to \"SomeChildName\""],"w":"$Name is shorthand for get_node(\"Name\"); it's how a script reaches another node in the tree."},
	{"l":2,"q":"Why does queue_free() exist instead of just always using free() directly?","o":["queue_free() defers the actual deletion to the end of the frame, which is safer while the tree is mid-update","queue_free() is just a slower synonym for free() with no real difference","free() only works on scenes, while queue_free() only works on scripts","queue_free() undoes a deletion instead of performing one"],"w":"Freeing a node immediately while something else is iterating over the tree (e.g. during a physics callback) can crash; queue_free() waits for a safe point."},
	{"l":2,"q":"What are node \"groups\" for?","o":["Tagging nodes so you can fetch or message all of them at once, e.g. get_tree().get_nodes_in_group(\"enemies\")","Making several nodes share one single collision shape","Grouping files together in the FileSystem dock","Compressing several scenes into one file on export"],"w":"Groups let you treat a loose set of nodes (like all enemies) as one collection without hard-coding references to each one."},
	{"l":2,"q":"You add extra child nodes to a scene instance at runtime with add_child(). Are those children saved back into the original .tscn file?","o":["No: only the nodes that exist in the saved scene are part of it; runtime children vanish when the instance is freed","Yes, every add_child() call permanently rewrites the .tscn file on disk","Yes, but only if the game is running in the editor, not in an exported build","No, and the scene file becomes corrupted every time this happens"],"w":"A scene file is a fixed template; children added in code exist only in that running instance, not in the file itself."},
	{"l":2,"q":"What does the % prefix on a node name (a \"unique name\" in the Scene dock) let you do?","o":["Reference that node from anywhere in the scene with %Name, instead of a long relative path","Make the node invisible by default when the scene loads","Mark the node as read-only so its script cannot change it","Automatically delete the node when the scene is saved"],"w":"Unique names give you a short, move-proof way to grab a node deep in the tree without $Parent/Parent/Child-style paths breaking when you reorganize."},
	{"l":2,"q":"A scene instance has \"editable children\" turned off by default in the Scene dock. What does that protect?","o":["It stops you from accidentally editing the internals of a reusable scene from every place it's instanced","It stops the scene from ever being instanced more than once","It prevents the scene from having any scripts attached to it","It hides the scene from the FileSystem dock entirely"],"w":"Without this, tweaking one instance's internals in the editor could look like it only affects that copy, while actually it protects the original template from accidental edits."},

	# ---------- 3 GDScript basics ----------
	{"l":3,"q":"What's the difference between var and const in GDScript?","o":["var can be reassigned later; const is fixed once at declaration and can never change","They are identical; const is just shorter to type","const can only hold numbers; var can hold any type","var is only for scripts; const is only for scenes"],"w":"Use const for values that should never change, like a fixed speed limit or a conversion factor."},
	{"l":3,"q":"What does var speed: float = 200.0 add compared to var speed = 200.0?","o":["An explicit type hint, so the editor and the compiler can catch a wrong-type assignment earlier","Nothing at all; the colon-type syntax is purely decorative and has zero effect","It makes the variable read-only, like const","It tells Godot to export the variable to the Inspector automatically"],"w":"Typed variables aren't required, but they catch mistakes sooner and make autocomplete and tooling more useful."},
	{"l":3,"q":"When is _ready() called on a node?","o":["Once, after the node and all its children have entered the scene tree","Every single frame, for as long as the node exists","Only when the node is about to be freed","Only if you call it manually from another script"],"w":"_ready() is the place to do one-time setup once you know the whole subtree under this node is built."},
	{"l":3,"q":"What does the delta parameter in _process(delta) represent?","o":["The time in seconds since the previous frame, so movement can be frame-rate independent","The number of pixels the mouse moved since the last frame","A random number used to add variety to animations","The total time the game has been running"],"w":"Multiplying a speed by delta keeps motion consistent whether the game runs at 30fps or 144fps."},
	{"l":3,"q":"What's the difference between _process(delta) and _physics_process(delta)?","o":["_physics_process runs at a fixed rate (60 times a second by default); _process runs once per rendered frame, which can vary","They are two names for exactly the same callback","_process only runs in 3D games; _physics_process only runs in 2D games","_physics_process runs only once total, at the very start of the game"],"w":"Movement and collision logic usually belongs in _physics_process, because its fixed step keeps physics stable."},
	{"l":3,"q":"What does @onready var sprite = $Sprite2D do?","o":["Waits until the node has entered the tree, then grabs a reference to the child named Sprite2D","Creates a brand new Sprite2D node and adds it as a child","Deletes the Sprite2D node as soon as the script loads","Makes the variable only readable from other scripts, never from this one"],"w":"@onready defers the assignment until just before _ready(), by which point $Sprite2D is guaranteed to exist."},
	{"l":3,"q":"What does @export var max_health: int = 100 do?","o":["Exposes max_health as an editable field in the Inspector for whoever places this node","Saves max_health permanently to a file the first time the game runs","Makes max_health update automatically over time, like an animation","Shares max_health with every other node in the project"],"w":"@export is how a script's variables become something a designer (or you) can tweak without opening the code."},
	{"l":3,"q":"What does func take_damage(amount: int) -> void: declare?","o":["A function named take_damage that takes an int argument and returns nothing","A variable named take_damage that stores a number","A signal named take_damage that other nodes can listen for","A new node type called take_damage"],"w":"func defines a function; the -> void (or any type) after the arguments documents what it returns, if anything."},

	# ---------- 4 Signals ----------
	{"l":4,"q":"What is a Godot \"signal,\" in one sentence?","o":["A message a node can emit so that other nodes, without being directly referenced, can react to it","A type of texture used only in UI nodes","A setting that controls how fast physics runs","A kind of file format for saving scenes"],"w":"Signals let one node announce \"this happened\" without needing to know who, if anyone, is listening."},
	{"l":4,"q":"How do you make a function run whenever a Button is pressed?","o":["Connect the button's pressed signal to your function, e.g. button.pressed.connect(_on_pressed)","Call the function directly inside _process(delta) every frame","Rename your function to \"pressed\" and Godot will find it automatically","Put the function inside the Button's own script file only, never your own"],"w":"Connecting a signal to a Callable is how nodes talk to each other without hard-wiring direct references."},
	{"l":4,"q":"How do you declare your own custom signal on a script?","o":["signal health_changed(new_health)","func health_changed(new_health):","@export signal health_changed(new_health)","const health_changed = \"signal\""],"w":"signal declares the event's name and, optionally, the arguments it will carry when emitted."},
	{"l":4,"q":"Once a signal is declared, how do you actually fire it?","o":["Call it like a method, e.g. health_changed.emit(new_health), or use emit_signal(\"health_changed\", new_health)","Signals fire automatically once per frame with no code needed","Set the signal's value using the = operator, like a variable","Call queue_free() on the node that declared it"],"w":"Declaring a signal only defines the event; you still have to emit it at the moment the event actually happens."},
	{"l":4,"q":"Why prefer a signal over just calling another node's method directly?","o":["It decouples the two nodes: the emitter doesn't need to know who's listening, or whether anyone is","It makes the code run faster in every single case, with no exceptions","Direct method calls are not allowed anywhere in GDScript","Signals are required before a script will even compile"],"w":"Direct calls tie two nodes together tightly; a signal lets the emitter stay reusable even if the listener changes."},
	{"l":4,"q":"Area2D has a built-in body_entered(body) signal. What does it fire for?","o":["Whenever a physics body enters that Area2D's shape, useful for triggers, pickups, and hazards","Whenever the Area2D itself moves to a new position","Once, the very first time the scene loads","Whenever the player presses a key on the keyboard"],"w":"Area2D doesn't produce solid collision response like a RigidBody2D would; it's built for detecting overlap instead."},
	{"l":4,"q":"You accidentally connect the same signal to the same function twice. What happens when the signal fires?","o":["The function runs twice for that one event, which can cause bugs like double damage being applied","Godot silently ignores the second connection and nothing changes","The game refuses to start until the duplicate connection is removed","The second connection overwrites the signal's name"],"w":"Godot allows duplicate connections unless you guard against it; a stray double-connect is a real, easy-to-miss bug."},
	{"l":4,"q":"What does await some_node.some_signal do inside a function?","o":["Pauses that function until some_signal fires on some_node, then resumes","Permanently blocks the entire game until the signal fires","Immediately disconnects some_signal so it can never fire again","Deletes some_node if the signal has not fired yet"],"w":"await lets you write \"wait for this to happen, then continue\" in a straight line instead of juggling callback functions."},

	# ---------- 5 Input & the process loop ----------
	{"l":5,"q":"What's the difference between Input.is_action_pressed(\"jump\") and the _input(event) callback?","o":["is_action_pressed polls the current held state at any moment you ask; _input reacts to discrete events as they arrive","They are exactly the same mechanism, just with different names","is_action_pressed only works for the mouse; _input only works for the keyboard","_input runs once per game session; is_action_pressed runs every frame no matter what"],"w":"Polling is handy for \"is the button held right now\" (like movement); events are handy for \"something just happened once\" (like a menu click)."},
	{"l":5,"q":"Where do named input actions like \"jump\" or \"move_left\" come from?","o":["The Input Map, defined once in Project Settings and mapped to one or more physical keys/buttons","They are hard-coded key names built into the engine and cannot be changed","They are created automatically the first time a key is pressed in-game","They only exist if you write them directly as string literals in every script"],"w":"The Input Map lets you rebind a key once in Project Settings instead of hunting through every script that checks for it."},
	{"l":5,"q":"What's the difference between _input(event) and _unhandled_input(event)?","o":["_unhandled_input only fires if nothing earlier (like a UI element) already consumed the event with accept_event()","_unhandled_input fires before _input, every single time","_input only works in 3D scenes; _unhandled_input only works in 2D scenes","They are two unrelated signals with nothing in common"],"w":"This lets UI capture a click first, and only lets it reach gameplay code if the UI didn't want it."},
	{"l":5,"q":"Why multiply movement by delta instead of using a fixed number of pixels per frame?","o":["So the object moves at the same real-world speed regardless of how many frames per second the game is running at","Because delta is always exactly 1, so it changes nothing","To make the object move faster for players with a faster monitor","Because GDScript requires every number in _process to be multiplied by something"],"w":"Without delta, the same code would make a 30fps player move at half the speed of a 60fps player."},
	{"l":5,"q":"On a CharacterBody2D, what does calling move_and_slide() do?","o":["Moves the body using its velocity property, sliding along any surfaces it collides with, and reports the collisions","Deletes the body and replaces it with a new one at a random position","Only works in 3D, never on a 2D node","Permanently disables all future collision detection for that body"],"w":"move_and_slide() reads the velocity you've set and handles the messy geometry of sliding along walls and floors for you."},
	{"l":5,"q":"What does set_process(false) do when called on a node?","o":["Stops that node's _process(delta) from being called every frame, without removing the node from the tree","Permanently deletes the node","Pauses the entire game, including every other node","Hides the node visually but keeps calling _process anyway"],"w":"It's a cheap way to turn off per-frame work for a node that doesn't currently need it, like an off-screen enemy."},
	{"l":5,"q":"What does Engine.get_frames_per_second() tell you?","o":["How many frames the game is currently rendering per second","How many physics steps have run since the game started","How many nodes currently exist in the scene tree","How much memory the current scene is using"],"w":"It's a quick way to check whether your game is actually hitting the frame rate you expect."},
	{"l":5,"q":"True or false: _physics_process always runs exactly once between every call to _process.","o":["False: depending on frame rate, _physics_process can run zero, one, or multiple times before the next _process call","True, this is guaranteed in every single case with no exceptions","True, but only on Windows; it differs on other platforms","False, because _physics_process never actually runs more than once per game"],"w":"If rendering falls behind, physics can \"catch up\" with more than one step between rendered frames; if it's running fast, it may skip a frame instead."},

	# ---------- 6 2D physics & collision ----------
	{"l":6,"q":"What's the practical difference between CharacterBody2D, RigidBody2D, and StaticBody2D?","o":["CharacterBody2D is script-driven movement (like a player); RigidBody2D is driven by the physics engine (like a crate); StaticBody2D never moves (like a wall)","They are three identical nodes with different icons and nothing else different","StaticBody2D is for 3D only; the other two are for 2D only","RigidBody2D cannot collide with anything, ever"],"w":"Picking the right body type decides who is in control: your code, the physics simulation, or nobody because it's fixed in place."},
	{"l":6,"q":"Why does a physics body need a CollisionShape2D child to actually collide with anything?","o":["The body node defines behavior, but the shape defines the actual geometry physics checks for overlap","CollisionShape2D is purely visual and has no effect on physics at all","A body collides with everything on screen automatically, with or without a shape","CollisionShape2D is only required for Area2D, never for physics bodies"],"w":"Without a shape, a physics body has no geometry to test against, so nothing will ever register a collision with it."},
	{"l":6,"q":"What makes Area2D different from a solid physics body like RigidBody2D?","o":["Area2D detects overlap (useful for triggers and pickups) but produces no physical collision response","Area2D is heavier to simulate than any other physics node","Area2D can only be used in 3D projects, never in 2D","Area2D automatically destroys anything that enters it"],"w":"An Area2D lets things pass straight through while still telling your code \"something just entered.\""},
	{"l":6,"q":"What's the difference between a collision layer and a collision mask?","o":["Layer describes what this object IS; mask describes what layers this object DETECTS","They are the exact same setting shown in two different panels","Layer only matters for 3D; mask only matters for 2D","Mask controls the object's color, and layer controls its shape"],"w":"A simple way to remember it: \"layer = what I am, mask = what I look for.\""},
	{"l":6,"q":"An Area2D emits body_entered(body) and an Area2D emits area_entered(area). What's the difference?","o":["body_entered fires for physics bodies (CharacterBody2D, RigidBody2D, StaticBody2D); area_entered fires for other Area2D nodes","They are two names for the exact same event with no real difference","body_entered only fires once per game; area_entered can fire repeatedly","area_entered fires for the player only; body_entered fires for enemies only"],"w":"Bodies and other areas are tracked separately, since an Area2D overlapping another Area2D is a different situation than a solid body passing through."},
	{"l":6,"q":"What's the difference between move_and_collide() and move_and_slide() on a physics body?","o":["move_and_collide moves once and stops dead on the first collision; move_and_slide keeps sliding along the surface it hit","They always produce identical movement in every situation","move_and_collide only works in 3D; move_and_slide only works in 2D","move_and_slide ignores collisions entirely and always passes through"],"w":"move_and_slide is usually what you want for characters walking along floors and walls; move_and_collide gives you more manual control over what happens at the moment of impact."},
	{"l":6,"q":"What does the KinematicCollision2D object returned by move_and_collide() give you?","o":["Details about the collision: the colliding body, the point of contact, and the surface normal","The exact pixel color of whatever was hit","A list of every node in the entire scene tree","The amount of memory the physics engine is using"],"w":"That detail is what lets you react differently depending on, say, whether you hit a wall versus a floor."},
	{"l":6,"q":"Physics in Godot normally runs at a fixed rate (default 60 times a second) regardless of the render frame rate. Why does that matter?","o":["It keeps physics behavior consistent and stable across machines with different display frame rates","It means physics never actually runs unless the display is also running at exactly 60fps","It makes every game run in exactly 60 real-world seconds no matter what","It has no actual effect on anything observable in the game"],"w":"Decoupling the physics step from the display's refresh rate avoids physics speeding up or slowing down just because a monitor is faster or slower."},

	# ---------- 7 UI / Control nodes ----------
	{"l":7,"q":"What is the Control node the base class for?","o":["Every 2D UI element: buttons, labels, panels, containers, and so on","Every 3D object in the game world","Every sound effect and music track","Every physics body in a 2D scene"],"w":"If it's meant to be a piece of user interface rather than part of the game world, it descends from Control."},
	{"l":7,"q":"What's the difference between a Control node's anchors and its offsets?","o":["Anchors are fractions of the parent's size the control's edges stick to; offsets are fixed pixel adjustments from those anchors","They are the same value, just measured in two different units with no functional difference","Anchors only apply in 3D projects; offsets only apply in 2D","Offsets control color; anchors control position"],"w":"Anchors are what let a UI element reposition sensibly when the window is resized, instead of staying glued to fixed pixel coordinates."},
	{"l":7,"q":"What do container nodes like VBoxContainer and HBoxContainer do for their children?","o":["Automatically arrange and resize their children in a row or column, instead of you positioning each one by hand","Hide their children from view entirely","Turn their children into physics bodies","Merge all their children into a single texture"],"w":"Containers save you from manually computing pixel positions every time a UI element is added, removed, or resized."},
	{"l":7,"q":"What do a Control node's size_flags (like \"Expand\" and \"Fill\") control?","o":["How that control grows or shrinks to share available space inside a container","Whether the control is visible at all","How fast the control's animations play","Which collision layer the control belongs to"],"w":"Inside a container, size flags decide who gets the extra space when the container is bigger than the sum of its children's minimum sizes."},
	{"l":7,"q":"What is a Theme resource used for?","o":["Applying a consistent look (fonts, colors, button styles) across many UI nodes at once","Storing the save file for a player's progress","Defining physics collision shapes for UI elements","Compiling the project into an executable"],"w":"Instead of styling every single button by hand, a Theme lets you set the look once and have it apply everywhere."},
	{"l":7,"q":"How do you run code when a Button is clicked?","o":["Connect its pressed signal to a function","Override the button's _process(delta) method directly on the Button node itself","Rename the button's text to the function's name","Set the button's \"on_click\" exported variable to a string of code"],"w":"Like most interactive nodes, Button communicates through signals rather than requiring you to poll it every frame."},
	{"l":7,"q":"What's the difference between Label and RichTextLabel?","o":["Label shows plain, unstyled text; RichTextLabel supports inline formatting like bold, color, and even embedded images","They render identically; RichTextLabel is only kept for backward compatibility","Label can only show numbers; RichTextLabel can only show letters","RichTextLabel cannot be resized, but Label can"],"w":"Reach for RichTextLabel when you need formatting inside the text itself, like a chat log with colored names."},
	{"l":7,"q":"What does a Control's mouse_filter property (Stop / Pass / Ignore) control?","o":["Whether that control blocks mouse input from reaching whatever is behind or below it","How many times a second the mouse position is checked","Whether the control can be seen by the camera","How the control's texture is filtered when scaled"],"w":"Set it to Ignore on something like a decorative overlay so clicks pass through to the buttons underneath it."},

	# ---------- 8 Resources & scenes-as-data ----------
	{"l":8,"q":"What is a Resource in Godot, at its core?","o":["A data container that can be saved to and loaded from disk, like a Texture, a Script, or your own custom data type","A node that must exist somewhere in the scene tree","A kind of signal used only for networking","A built-in physics body type"],"w":"Resources hold data, not behavior-in-a-tree; a Texture2D and a PackedScene are both resources, for example."},
	{"l":8,"q":"What does a .tscn file actually compile down to when Godot loads it?","o":["A PackedScene resource, which can be instantiated into real nodes with .instantiate()","A plain text file with no structured meaning to the engine","An executable program that runs on its own","A single image that is drawn directly to the screen"],"w":"A PackedScene is the packaged, loadable form of a scene; instancing it is what turns it into live nodes."},
	{"l":8,"q":"What's the real difference between preload(\"res://thing.tres\") and load(\"res://thing.tres\")?","o":["preload resolves at compile time, baking the resource in; load resolves at runtime, when that line actually executes","They are exactly the same function under two different names","preload only works for scripts; load only works for scenes","load caches nothing, but preload caches everything forever with no way to reload it"],"w":"preload is handy for something you always need; load is handy when the path is only known while the game is running."},
	{"l":8,"q":"What's the difference between a .tres file and a .res file?","o":[".tres is Godot's human-readable text format for a resource; .res is the binary format","They store completely different kinds of data with no overlap at all","Only .tres can be loaded in an exported game; .res only works in the editor","There is no difference; they are interchangeable file extensions for the same bytes"],"w":"Text resources are easier to diff in version control; binary resources are smaller and a bit faster to load."},
	{"l":8,"q":"You give two different nodes the same @export var item: Resource with no default duplication. What's the classic bug this sets up?","o":["Both nodes can end up sharing and unintentionally mutating the very same underlying Resource instance","Nothing can go wrong, since every Resource is automatically unique per node","The game will refuse to start until each node gets a different resource type","The second node silently gets a copy that always lags one frame behind"],"w":"Unless you explicitly duplicate() a shared default resource, editing it through one node can quietly change it for every other node pointing at the same instance."},
	{"l":8,"q":"Why would you write a small custom Resource script (e.g. class_name ItemData extends Resource) instead of a plain Dictionary for game data like items?","o":["It gets typed fields, Inspector editing, and can be saved/loaded as its own .tres file, with real autocomplete and error checking","Dictionaries cannot be used anywhere in GDScript at all","Resources run noticeably faster than dictionaries for every possible operation","A Resource script is required before any @export variable will work"],"w":"A typed Resource turns \"a bag of loosely related values\" into something the editor and the type checker both understand."},
	{"l":8,"q":"What problem do stable resource references via uid:// (alongside the res:// path) help solve?","o":["Moving or renaming a file no longer silently breaks every reference that pointed at its old path","They make every resource load twice as fast, with no other effect","They replace the need for the FileSystem dock entirely","They are only used for audio files, nothing else"],"w":"A path-only reference breaks the moment you reorganize folders; a stable ID survives the move."},
	{"l":8,"q":"Why can treating a .tscn scene file as pure data (rather than as compiled code) be useful for a level editor or a mod system?","o":["Scenes can be built, saved, and loaded as data at runtime without recompiling the game","Scenes cannot be loaded at runtime at all, only in the editor","Treating a scene as data disables all of its scripts permanently","It only works for 3D scenes, never for 2D ones"],"w":"Because a PackedScene is just data you can load dynamically, letting players or tools build content without touching source code becomes possible."},

	# ---------- 9 Animation ----------
	{"l":9,"q":"What is an AnimationPlayer for?","o":["Recording and playing back keyframed tracks that change properties (position, color, visibility, and more) over time","Playing background music only, nothing visual","Simulating physics collisions between two bodies","Compiling the project for export"],"w":"It's a timeline editor: you record how values should change over time, and it plays that back on demand."},
	{"l":9,"q":"What's the main use case for create_tween() compared to an AnimationPlayer?","o":["Writing a short, one-off interpolation directly in code, without authoring a timeline in the editor","Permanently replacing AnimationPlayer for every kind of animation","Playing audio files exclusively","Only working in 3D scenes, never 2D or UI"],"w":"A Tween is great for quick procedural effects, like a button that scales up on hover, without needing a dedicated timeline asset."},
	{"l":9,"q":"What is an AnimationTree mainly used for?","o":["Blending between multiple animations (like walk and run) and driving animation state transitions with logic","Drawing a visual tree of every node in the scene","Storing a list of every sound effect in the project","Replacing the need for an AnimationPlayer's keyframes entirely"],"w":"AnimationTree sits on top of AnimationPlayer's clips to smoothly mix and switch between them based on game state."},
	{"l":9,"q":"What does AnimationPlayer's animation_finished signal let you do?","o":["Run code exactly when a specific animation finishes playing, like triggering the next action in a sequence","Pause the animation partway through, every time, automatically","Delete the AnimationPlayer node once it fires","Rename the animation that just finished"],"w":"It's how you chain \"play this, then when it's done, do that\" without polling the animation's progress every frame."},
	{"l":9,"q":"What is a \"call method\" track inside an AnimationPlayer timeline for?","o":["Calling a specific function on a node at an exact point during the animation's playback","Deleting a node partway through the animation automatically","Changing the animation's name while it plays","Speeding up every other track in the same animation"],"w":"It's useful for timing gameplay logic (like spawning a hit effect) to an exact frame of a visual animation."},
	{"l":9,"q":"What does the \"transition\" / \"ease\" setting on a Tween step control?","o":["The curve the interpolation follows over time (e.g. linear, ease-in, bounce), not just the start and end values","Whether the tween repeats forever or only once","Which node the tween is attached to","The resolution of the texture being animated"],"w":"The same start and end values can look completely different depending on the easing curve used to get from one to the other."},
	{"l":9,"q":"What's the difference between Sprite2D and AnimatedSprite2D?","o":["Sprite2D shows one static texture; AnimatedSprite2D cycles through a SpriteFrames set of frames to show motion","They are identical nodes, just with two different names for historical reasons","AnimatedSprite2D can only be used for UI, never for gameplay characters","Sprite2D requires an AnimationPlayer; AnimatedSprite2D never does"],"w":"If you need frame-by-frame sprite animation (like a walk cycle made of individual images), AnimatedSprite2D is built for exactly that."},
	{"l":9,"q":"What does an AnimationPlayer's playback speed_scale property do?","o":["Speeds up or slows down the whole animation's playback relative to normal speed","Changes how large the animated sprite appears on screen","Changes the pitch of any sound the animation triggers","Permanently bakes a new set of keyframes at the new speed"],"w":"A speed_scale of 2.0 plays the same animation back twice as fast without re-authoring any keyframes."},

	# ---------- 10 Autoloads & global state ----------
	{"l":10,"q":"What is an \"autoload\" (singleton) in Godot?","o":["A script or scene that's always loaded and accessible globally by name, for the whole game's lifetime","A node that automatically deletes itself after one frame","A setting that makes the game start automatically without pressing play","A special kind of signal that fires only once per session"],"w":"Autoloads are the standard way to have something (like game state or an audio manager) exist everywhere without manually wiring references between scenes."},
	{"l":10,"q":"Where do you set up an autoload?","o":["Project Settings > Autoload, giving the script or scene a name and a path","By adding @autoload above the script's class_name line","Inside the Inspector of any individual node","By naming a file \"autoload.gd\" anywhere in the project"],"w":"Project Settings is the one place that registers a script or scene to be instanced automatically at startup."},
	{"l":10,"q":"Autoloads are initialized before the main scene loads. Why does that matter?","o":["Code in your main scene can safely assume an autoload like GameState already exists and is ready to use","It means autoloads cannot have any variables at all, only functions","It means the main scene actually loads first, and this ordering guarantee is backwards","It has no practical effect on anything, ever"],"w":"That ordering guarantee is what makes it safe for any scene to reach for GameState.something in its own _ready()."},
	{"l":10,"q":"If you've registered an autoload named GameState, how do you access it from any script in the project?","o":["Just reference it directly by name, like GameState.score += 1","You must call get_node(\"/root/Main/GameState\") every single time, with the full path","Autoloads cannot be accessed from scripts at all, only from the editor","You have to re-instance a new copy of it in every scene that needs it"],"w":"Once registered, the autoload's name becomes a globally available identifier, no manual lookup required."},
	{"l":10,"q":"What's a real downside of putting too much into global autoload state?","o":["It can create tight, hidden coupling between unrelated systems, making the code harder to test and reason about","Autoloads make the game run slower by a fixed, large amount no matter what","Godot limits you to exactly one autoload per project","Autoloads cannot hold any exported variables"],"w":"A global that everything reads and writes can turn into a tangle where changing one system quietly breaks another."},
	{"l":10,"q":"Why might you use a persistent autoload node instead of relying on the current scene alone when changing scenes?","o":["An autoload survives scene changes, so data like score or inventory isn't wiped out when you load a new scene","The current scene's nodes always persist automatically across scene changes anyway","Only autoloads are allowed to have scripts attached to them","Scene changes are impossible unless an autoload exists"],"w":"Regular scene nodes are replaced wholesale on a scene change; an autoload is not, which is exactly why it's a natural home for state that should survive."},
	{"l":10,"q":"Why might a global EventBus autoload emit signals instead of game systems calling each other's methods directly?","o":["It lets unrelated systems react to the same event without needing direct references to one another","Signals on an autoload are the only signals that are allowed to carry arguments","It's required, because autoloads cannot have their own custom signals","It automatically logs every event to a file on disk"],"w":"A shared signal bus keeps systems decoupled even when many different parts of the game care about the same event."},
	{"l":10,"q":"Where does centralizing save/load logic in a single autoload (rather than scattering file I/O across many scripts) help most?","o":["There is one place that knows the full shape of the save data, so it's easier to keep correct and to extend later","It makes the save file itself load noticeably faster on every platform","It is the only way Godot allows you to write a file at all","It automatically encrypts the save file with no extra code"],"w":"When save logic lives in one place, adding a new field to the save format means touching one script, not hunting through the whole project."},

	# ---------- 11 State machines & architecture ----------
	{"l":11,"q":"Why prefer an enum-based state machine (IDLE, RUNNING, JUMPING, ...) over a pile of separate booleans like is_jumping, is_running?","o":["Only one state is possible at a time by construction, instead of booleans that can silently contradict each other","Booleans run measurably slower than enums in every GDScript program","Enums are required before a script will compile in Godot","There is no real difference; it's purely a stylistic preference with zero practical effect"],"w":"With scattered booleans, nothing stops is_jumping and is_running from both being true at once in a way that makes no sense; an enum state can't be two things simultaneously."},
	{"l":11,"q":"What does a basic finite state machine pattern look like in GDScript?","o":["A match statement (or similar branching) on a current state enum, where each branch defines that state's behavior and its allowed transitions","A single very long if/else chain with no state variable at all","A dictionary that maps every possible input directly to a sound effect","A scene that contains no scripts whatsoever"],"w":"match state: IDLE: ... RUNNING: ... is a clean, readable way to keep each state's logic in one clearly labeled place."},
	{"l":11,"q":"What's a benefit of modeling each state as its own object or script, rather than one giant script with a state enum and endless branches?","o":["Each state's logic is isolated, so adding or changing one state is less likely to break the others","It's the only way Godot allows states to be represented at all","It automatically makes every state run in parallel at the same time","It removes the need for a state variable entirely"],"w":"As the number of states and transitions grows, separating them into their own small units keeps any one state's logic from spilling into the others."},
	{"l":11,"q":"In GDScript, what does a match statement let you write cleanly?","o":["A single value compared against several possible patterns, running different code for each match","A loop that repeats forever with no exit condition","A way to declare multiple variables of different types at once","A built-in physics collision check"],"w":"match is GDScript's structured alternative to a long chain of if/elif comparisons against the same variable."},
	{"l":11,"q":"Why might a state machine favor transitioning in response to signals (like took_damage or reached_goal) rather than polling conditions every frame?","o":["The transition logic runs exactly when the relevant event happens, instead of re-checking conditions on every single frame regardless of relevance","Signals are the only way GDScript can change a variable's value","Polling for conditions is not possible anywhere in Godot","It guarantees the state machine will never have more than two states"],"w":"Event-driven transitions can be clearer and cheaper than constantly asking \"has X happened yet?\" inside _process every frame."},
	{"l":11,"q":"Godot's node/scene system nudges you toward composition over deep inheritance. What does that mean in practice?","o":["You build behavior by combining small, focused nodes as children, rather than one big class inheriting from another big class","Every script must inherit directly from Node with no other base class allowed","Inheritance is completely disabled in GDScript","Composition means every node must have exactly one child, no more and no fewer"],"w":"A Player node built from a Hurtbox, an AnimationPlayer, and a State node is easier to rearrange than one monolithic PlayerEverything class."},
	{"l":11,"q":"What's the idea behind a \"component\" pattern, like giving an enemy a HealthComponent child node?","o":["Reusable, self-contained behavior (like tracking and losing health) lives in its own node that any actor can have as a child","Every component must be written in a completely different programming language","Components can only ever be added in the Godot editor, never through code","A component is just another name for an autoload"],"w":"Instead of copy-pasting health-tracking code into every actor's script, a HealthComponent can be dropped onto a player, an enemy, or a destructible crate alike."},
	{"l":11,"q":"What's a practical downside of one Player.gd script handling movement, animation, inventory, combat, and dialogue all at once?","o":["Any change to one responsibility risks breaking an unrelated one, and the script becomes harder to read and test","GDScript enforces a hard line limit, so the script simply will not run","It automatically runs slower the more responsibilities a single script has","It becomes impossible to attach the script to more than one node"],"w":"A script that does everything tends to accumulate tangled dependencies between its unrelated parts, which is exactly what makes bugs hard to isolate later."},

	# ---------- 12 3D basics ----------
	{"l":12,"q":"What's the relationship between Node2D, Node3D, and Control?","o":["All three are base classes for different kinds of content: flat 2D objects, objects in 3D space, and UI elements respectively","Node3D is simply Node2D with an extra unused property","Control is a special case of Node3D used only for 3D menus","They are interchangeable and can always be swapped for one another with no changes"],"w":"Picking the right base class gives each kind of object the transform and behavior appropriate to it, 2D, 3D, or on-screen UI."},
	{"l":12,"q":"A 3D scene can contain several Camera3D nodes. How does Godot know which one is actually rendering the view?","o":["Exactly one Camera3D is marked \"current\" at a time (directly, or the first one found if none is set)","Godot renders the view from all cameras at once, blended together","The camera closest to the world origin is always used, with no way to change that","Camera3D nodes cannot coexist; a scene may only ever contain one"],"w":"Marking a camera current (or calling make_current()) is how you switch which viewpoint the player actually sees, e.g. between a gameplay camera and a cutscene camera."},
	{"l":12,"q":"What's the relationship between a MeshInstance3D node and a Mesh resource?","o":["MeshInstance3D is the node that places a reusable Mesh resource (the actual geometry and material data) into the scene","A Mesh resource can only exist attached to exactly one MeshInstance3D, ever","MeshInstance3D generates a brand new unique mesh shape every time it's placed","They are two unrelated systems with no connection to each other"],"w":"The same Mesh resource (say, a barrel model) can be reused by many MeshInstance3D nodes scattered through a level, without duplicating the geometry data."},
	{"l":12,"q":"What's the practical difference between DirectionalLight3D, OmniLight3D, and SpotLight3D?","o":["DirectionalLight3D simulates parallel light like the sun; OmniLight3D radiates in all directions from a point; SpotLight3D is a cone from a point","They are three different names for literally the same lighting behavior","OmniLight3D only works outdoors; DirectionalLight3D only works indoors","SpotLight3D cannot cast any shadows, unlike the other two"],"w":"Picking the right light type matches the real-world behavior you're trying to simulate: sunlight, a bare bulb, or a flashlight beam."},
	{"l":12,"q":"What does a WorldEnvironment node's Environment resource control?","o":["Scene-wide visual settings like the sky, ambient light, fog, and tonemapping","The exact position of every light in the scene individually","The physics gravity direction for every RigidBody3D","The collision layers available in the project"],"w":"It's the place to set the overall mood and look of a level — sky, fog, exposure — separately from any one object's own material."},
	{"l":12,"q":"Just like a 2D physics body needs a CollisionShape2D, what does a 3D physics body need to actually collide with things?","o":["A CollisionShape3D child defining its collision geometry","Nothing: 3D physics bodies collide using their visible mesh automatically","A Camera3D pointed directly at the body","An AnimationPlayer attached to the same node"],"w":"The visible MeshInstance3D and the collidable CollisionShape3D are separate concerns; a body needs the shape to participate in physics at all."},
	{"l":12,"q":"What does a Transform3D (built from a Basis and an origin) represent for a 3D node?","o":["Its position, rotation, and scale combined into a single transform","Only its color and material, nothing about position","Only how fast the node is currently moving","The node's name and script path"],"w":"Every placement, rotation, and resizing of a 3D object comes down to adjusting this one transform."},
	{"l":12,"q":"What is a Viewport (or SubViewport) useful for?","o":["Rendering a scene (or part of one) to a texture, which can then be reused elsewhere, like a security-camera feed on a screen in the game world","Storing the project's export settings","Running a second, completely independent copy of the whole game","Replacing the need for any Camera3D in the scene"],"w":"A SubViewport lets you render something once and reuse the result as a texture, instead of needing it drawn directly to the main screen."},

	# ---------- 13 Shaders & visual effects ----------
	{"l":13,"q":"What is a shader, in plain terms?","o":["A small GPU program that decides how something is drawn, pixel by pixel or vertex by vertex","A GDScript function that runs once when the game starts","A file format used only for saving player progress","A kind of signal used for networking"],"w":"Shaders run on the graphics card itself, which is what lets them do per-pixel or per-vertex work fast enough for real-time rendering."},
	{"l":13,"q":"What does the shader_type declaration at the top of a .gdshader file do?","o":["Tells Godot what kind of thing this shader draws: canvas_item (2D), spatial (3D), particles, and so on","Sets how many times per second the shader runs","Decides which GPU brand the shader is allowed to run on","Has no real effect; it exists only for documentation"],"w":"Different contexts (a 2D sprite vs. a 3D surface) expose different built-in variables, so the shader has to declare which one it's writing for."},
	{"l":13,"q":"What does a shader's fragment() function do?","o":["Runs once per pixel being drawn, typically to set that pixel's final COLOR","Runs once, total, for the entire draw call","Only runs when the mouse hovers over the object","Replaces the need for a vertex() function in every shader"],"w":"fragment() is where per-pixel effects (tinting, transparency, distortion) actually happen."},
	{"l":13,"q":"What is a uniform variable for in a shader?","o":["Exposing a parameter (like a color or strength value) that can be set from the material or from GDScript, without rewriting the shader","Declaring a variable that changes a different value for every single pixel","Storing the shader's compiled machine code","Permanently disabling that part of the shader"],"w":"Uniforms are how a shader stays reusable — tweak the uniform's value per-material instead of writing a near-duplicate shader for every variation."},
	{"l":13,"q":"What does a shader's vertex() function let you change?","o":["The position (and other per-vertex data) of the mesh's vertices before they're rasterized into pixels","The final on-screen color of each pixel","The name of the texture being sampled","The project's physics gravity setting"],"w":"Classic effects like waving grass or a wobbling blob move actual vertex positions, which is vertex() territory, not fragment()."},
	{"l":13,"q":"When would you reach for a ShaderMaterial instead of a built-in StandardMaterial3D?","o":["When you need a custom visual effect the built-in material options can't produce, like a dissolve or a toon outline","Every single mesh in Godot is required to use a ShaderMaterial; there is no alternative","ShaderMaterial is only valid on 2D nodes, never on 3D ones","StandardMaterial3D cannot be assigned any texture at all"],"w":"Built-in materials cover most common surfaces well; a custom shader is for the effects that fall outside what they can express."},
	{"l":13,"q":"In a canvas_item fragment shader, what does writing to the built-in COLOR variable do?","o":["Sets the final output color (including alpha) for that pixel of the 2D element being drawn","Changes the color of every other sprite in the scene at once","Sets the background color of the entire viewport","Has no visible effect; COLOR is read-only"],"w":"COLOR is the output the GPU actually uses for that pixel, so writing to it is how a 2D shader changes what you see."},
	{"l":13,"q":"Why does shader complexity matter for performance even though it \"just\" runs on the GPU?","o":["A fragment shader runs once per pixel on screen, so expensive math there is multiplied across millions of pixels every frame","Shaders have no measurable performance cost at all, by design","Shader cost only matters on mobile devices, never on desktop","A shader only ever runs once, no matter how large the screen is"],"w":"A shader that's cheap per-pixel can still add up to a real cost once you multiply it by every pixel on a large, high-resolution screen."},

	# ---------- 14 Performance & optimization ----------
	{"l":14,"q":"Why cache a get_node() (or $) lookup in a variable instead of calling it fresh every single frame inside _process?","o":["Repeating the tree lookup every frame is wasted work when the reference doesn't change; caching it once avoids that cost","get_node() can only ever be called one single time in the entire game's lifetime","$NodeName does not work at all inside _process, only inside _ready","Caching changes what node the reference points to over time"],"w":"If the target node isn't going anywhere, looking it up once in @onready or _ready and reusing the reference is simply less repeated work."},
	{"l":14,"q":"What problem does object pooling solve?","o":["The repeated cost of instancing and freeing many short-lived objects (like bullets), by reusing existing instances instead","It makes textures load faster from disk","It replaces the need for any collision shapes","It automatically reduces a texture's resolution"],"w":"Constantly creating and destroying nodes (like bullets in a bullet-hell game) has real overhead; recycling a pool of them avoids repeating that cost every time one is needed."},
	{"l":14,"q":"How does the number of active physics bodies in a scene typically affect physics_process cost?","o":["More bodies generally means more collision checks and more work each physics step, so cost tends to scale up with the count","The cost is always exactly the same no matter how many bodies exist","More bodies always make physics run faster due to better GPU batching","Physics cost depends only on screen resolution, not body count"],"w":"Collision detection and resolution scale with how many things there are to check against each other, which is why physics-heavy scenes need the body count watched."},
	{"l":14,"q":"What does \"batching\" draw calls generally mean for performance?","o":["Merging multiple similar draw operations into fewer GPU calls reduces per-call overhead and tends to raise frame rate","It means drawing every object one extra time for safety","It only affects audio playback, not rendering","It disables all visual effects to save time"],"w":"Each draw call has its own overhead; combining many small draws into fewer larger ones is a common way engines claw back performance."},
	{"l":14,"q":"What is the Profiler panel in the Godot editor for?","o":["Measuring where time is actually going each frame (scripts, physics, rendering, and more), instead of guessing","Writing and editing GDScript code","Exporting the project to different platforms","Managing which autoloads are registered"],"w":"Guessing at a performance problem wastes time; the profiler shows you, frame by frame, where the time is actually spent."},
	{"l":14,"q":"What is a VisibleOnScreenNotifier2D (or 3D) useful for?","o":["Telling your code when a node enters or leaves the camera's view, so you can skip work on things nobody can see","Making a node completely invisible forever, with no way to show it again","Forcing a node to always render on top of everything else","Replacing the need for a Camera node entirely"],"w":"Why animate or process an enemy that's far off-screen? This node gives you a clean signal to pause that work until it's actually visible again."},
	{"l":14,"q":"Instancing a complex scene at runtime has some cost. Why is this relevant to deciding between \"instance a new one each time\" and \"keep a pool of pre-instanced ones\"?","o":["If something is created and destroyed very frequently (bullets, particles, hit effects), that repeated instancing cost can add up and hurt frame rate","Instancing a scene always costs exactly zero time, so pooling never matters","Pooling only matters for 2D scenes, never for 3D ones","Scenes cannot be instanced more than once per second by design"],"w":"A single instantiate() for a rarely-spawned boss is a non-issue; doing it hundreds of times a second for bullets is where it starts to show up in the profiler."},
	{"l":14,"q":"You suspect your game is GPU-bound rather than CPU-bound. What's a sensible way to check?","o":["Use the profiler/monitors to compare time spent in rendering versus scripting and physics","Guess based on how the game looks, without measuring anything","Assume it's always GPU-bound, since that's true for most games by default","Reduce the window size, since that always fixes a GPU bottleneck permanently"],"w":"\"GPU-bound\" and \"CPU-bound\" are specific, measurable claims; the profiler is what turns a guess into an actual diagnosis."},

	# ---------- 15 Multiplayer & networking ----------
	{"l":15,"q":"What does Godot's high-level multiplayer API (built on top of ENet by default) give you over raw sockets?","o":["Ready-made peer connections, RPCs, and replication tools, instead of having to build networking primitives from scratch","Guaranteed zero network latency between any two players","A single-player-only mode with no networking at all","Automatic matchmaking with strangers over the public internet"],"w":"The high-level API handles the plumbing of peer connections and message routing so you can focus on what to replicate and when."},
	{"l":15,"q":"In a typical Godot multiplayer setup, what's the role of the \"server\" peer among all the connected peers?","o":["It's usually the authority that other peers' actions are routed through or validated against","It is always the peer with the fastest internet connection, chosen automatically","It renders every other peer's screen for them remotely","It only exists while the game is paused"],"w":"Having one agreed-upon authority (even a dedicated server, or just the host) avoids every peer disagreeing about what actually happened."},
	{"l":15,"q":"What does the @rpc annotation on a function let you do?","o":["Mark that function as callable remotely on other connected peers, not just locally","Make the function run twice as fast on every peer","Automatically translate the function's text into other languages","Disable the function entirely once multiplayer starts"],"w":"Without @rpc, a function call only affects the local peer; with it, you can trigger that call on other peers across the network."},
	{"l":15,"q":"What does set_multiplayer_authority() on a node decide?","o":["Which peer is allowed to control that node's authoritative state", "Which peer can see that node at all, hiding it from everyone else", "Which texture that node uses when rendered", "How many physics layers that node occupies"],"w":"Authority answers the question \"whose version of this node's state is the one everyone else should trust?\""},
	{"l":15,"q":"What is a MultiplayerSpawner node for?","o":["Automatically replicating the creation (and removal) of nodes across connected peers","Spawning enemies locally with no relation to networking at all","Rendering spawn-point markers in the editor only","Generating random terrain for a 3D level"],"w":"Instead of manually telling every peer \"someone just created X,\" a MultiplayerSpawner handles replicating that creation for you."},
	{"l":15,"q":"What is a MultiplayerSynchronizer node for?","o":["Automatically replicating chosen properties (like position) of a node from its authority to other peers","Keeping every peer's framerate perfectly identical","Synchronizing audio playback timing only","Replacing the need for any RPC calls in the whole project"],"w":"Rather than writing an RPC for every single property update, a MultiplayerSynchronizer can replicate a configured set of properties automatically."},
	{"l":15,"q":"Why would a script check is_multiplayer_authority() before applying movement to a node?","o":["So only the peer that actually owns/controls that node applies the authoritative movement, instead of every peer fighting over it","The check is required before any node can be drawn on screen at all","It toggles whether the node is visible to that specific peer","It has no real effect on multiplayer behavior"],"w":"Without that check, every peer might try to move the same node independently, producing conflicting, jittery results instead of one agreed position."},
	{"l":15,"q":"In simple terms, what problem does client-side prediction try to solve in networked games?","o":["Network latency means a player's own input would otherwise feel delayed; predicting the result locally first, then correcting if wrong, hides that delay","It eliminates the need for any server or authority at all","It guarantees there will never be any desync between players","It only matters for turn-based games, never action games"],"w":"Waiting for a server round-trip before showing your own character move would feel laggy; predicting locally and reconciling later is how many action games hide that lag."},

	# ---------- 16 Export & platform quirks ----------
	{"l":16,"q":"What does export_presets.cfg store?","o":["Your project's per-platform export configuration (which platform, what settings, where the output goes)","The source code of every script in the project","The save data for whoever last played the game","A list of every signal connection in the project"],"w":"It's generated once you configure an export target in the editor, and it's what lets export settings be reproduced without reconfiguring by hand."},
	{"l":16,"q":"Why must installed export templates match the editor's version (e.g. 4.7 templates for a 4.7 editor)?","o":["A mismatched version can fail to export at all, or produce a broken build, since the templates are the actual runtime being packaged","Templates are purely cosmetic and version matching is only a suggestion","Export templates are automatically updated the moment a new editor version is installed","Only the Windows export target cares about template version; others don't"],"w":"The export template IS the engine binary that will run your exported game, so an editor/template mismatch is a real, not cosmetic, problem."},
	{"l":16,"q":"Why does the Web export need special COOP/COEP HTTP headers to use multithreading?","o":["Browsers require cross-origin isolation (via those headers) before allowing SharedArrayBuffer, which threads depend on","Those headers are required for every website regardless of Godot","They control the page's visual theme, not its security model","They are only relevant to the Android export, not Web"],"w":"Without the right headers from the hosting server, a threaded Web export can fail to run even though the exact same build works locally."},
	{"l":16,"q":"Why does the total size of a Web-exported build matter more than it might for a desktop build?","o":["Everything has to download over the network before the game can start, so a larger build means a longer wait for the player","Web exports have no size limit of any kind, unlike every other platform","File size only affects loading speed on mobile phones, never on desktop browsers","Godot automatically compresses Web exports to zero extra size"],"w":"A desktop installer is downloaded once and then just runs; a web build's assets often have to be fetched before the player sees anything at all."},
	{"l":16,"q":"What is config/features in project.godot (e.g. PackedStringArray(\"4.7\", \"Forward Plus\")) used for?","o":["Recording which engine version and rendering method the project was set up with, for compatibility tracking","Listing every gameplay feature the player has unlocked so far","Controlling which export platforms are even allowed","Storing the list of installed export templates"],"w":"It's project metadata about what the project itself expects, not something players see or export settings read directly."},
	{"l":16,"q":"What's the practical difference between exporting with a debug template versus a release template?","o":["Debug builds include extra debugging/profiling support and are easier to troubleshoot; release builds are the leaner build meant to actually ship","They produce byte-for-byte identical output every time","Debug templates only work on Windows; release templates only work on consoles","Release builds always include the full editor inside them"],"w":"You develop and test against a debug export for the extra information it gives you, then ship a release export because it's smaller and faster."},
	{"l":16,"q":"Why do platform-specific permissions (like Android's camera or storage permissions) need to be explicitly declared in the export settings?","o":["Each platform enforces its own permission model, and a feature that needs a permission you didn't declare can fail at runtime on real devices","Permissions declared on one platform automatically apply to every other platform too","Godot requests every possible permission automatically by default, whether used or not","Declaring permissions is purely optional and never affects whether a feature works"],"w":"A feature that works fine in the editor can still fail silently on a real device if the platform-specific permission it needs was never declared in the export preset."},
	{"l":16,"q":"Why is it worth actually testing an exported build on the target platform, instead of only testing in the editor?","o":["Rendering, input, file access, and performance can all behave differently once exported, especially across different platforms","The exported build is always byte-for-byte identical in behavior to running in the editor","Testing exported builds is never necessary if the editor preview looked correct","Export-only bugs cannot exist in Godot by design"],"w":"The editor's preview is a convenience, not a guarantee; some bugs (web threading, mobile permissions, platform-specific input) only ever show up in the real exported build."},

	# ---------- 17 Real-world engineering lessons ----------
	{"l":17,"q":"Two people each edit the same .tscn scene file in separate branches and merge. What commonly goes wrong?","o":["Scene files are structured text, but a merge tool can still produce a broken or semantically nonsensical scene that no longer opens cleanly","Godot automatically prevents two people from ever editing the same scene","Merging two .tscn files is always completely safe, with no special care needed","The second person's changes are silently and safely discarded with no conflict at all"],"w":"Because a .tscn is really a serialized tree with cross-references, a naive text merge can leave it internally inconsistent even when it \"merges\" without flagging a conflict."},
	{"l":17,"q":"@onready var enemy = $Path/To/Enemy silently resolves to null because the path is wrong. What's the actual risk?","o":["Later code that calls a method on enemy crashes at runtime with a null reference error, often far from the real mistake","Godot refuses to even start the game until every @onready path is manually verified","The variable automatically falls back to a default enemy node","Nothing: GDScript always provides a harmless placeholder object instead of null"],"w":"A silently-null reference often doesn't fail where the real mistake is (the wrong path); it fails later, wherever that reference finally gets used."},
	{"l":17,"q":"A script calls get_node() on a sibling in its own _init() instead of _ready(). What commonly goes wrong?","o":["The sibling may not have entered the scene tree yet at that point, so the lookup can fail or return something unexpected","_init() always runs after every node in the scene is fully ready, so this is always safe","get_node() behaves identically no matter which lifecycle method calls it","This only matters in exported builds, never in the editor"],"w":"_ready() is the point where you can trust the surrounding tree is built; reaching for siblings earlier than that is a classic source of \"works sometimes\" bugs."},
	{"l":17,"q":"A gameplay signal gets connected twice by mistake (e.g. once in _ready() and once more somewhere else), and now damage is applied twice per hit. What's the real lesson?","o":["A duplicate connection isn't rejected automatically, so guarding against reconnecting (or disconnecting first) is sometimes your responsibility","Godot always prevents a signal from ever being connected to the same function twice","The bug can only happen in multiplayer games, never in single-player","Signals silently ignore the second emit, so this specific bug is actually impossible"],"w":"This is exactly the kind of bug that's invisible by reading the connecting line alone; it only shows up once you watch the handler actually fire twice."},
	{"l":17,"q":"A script reaches a deeply nested sibling with a long absolute path like $\"/root/Main/UI/HUD/HealthBar\". What's the risk of that approach?","o":["Reorganizing the scene tree later silently breaks that path, and the failure shows up far from where the reorganization happened","Absolute paths are always faster than relative ones, with no downside","Godot does not allow absolute node paths at all, so this would not compile","This approach is the officially recommended best practice with no caveats"],"w":"A long, rigid path couples a script to the exact current shape of the tree; a signal, a group, or an @export NodePath tends to survive refactors better."},
	{"l":17,"q":"Code inside a physics collision callback calls queue_free() on a colliding body directly and immediately frees it with free() instead. What can go wrong?","o":["Freeing a node immediately while the physics engine is still mid-callback on it can cause errors or crashes; call_deferred/queue_free exist to avoid exactly this","free() and queue_free() always behave identically in every context","Nothing: it is always perfectly safe to free() any node at any time","The physics engine automatically postpones every free() call on its own"],"w":"This is precisely why queue_free() (or call_deferred) exists: to avoid pulling a node out from under code that's still actively using it."},
	{"l":17,"q":"A character's movement code splits its logic between _process(delta) and _physics_process(delta), and the movement looks subtly jittery. What's the likely cause?","o":["Mixing a variable-rate callback with a fixed-rate one for the same motion can desynchronize the visible position from the physics step","Jitter in Godot is always caused by a graphics driver bug and nothing in the project's own code","_process and _physics_process always run in perfect lockstep, so this cannot be the cause","Jitter only ever happens in exported builds, never in the editor"],"w":"Keeping movement and collision logic together in _physics_process (and only reading the result in _process for smoothing) avoids fighting between two different update rates."},
	{"l":17,"q":"A bug report just says \"it crashes sometimes.\" Before trying to fix anything, what's the actually useful next step?","o":["Get (or build) a minimal, reliable way to reproduce it, since a fix for a bug you can't reproduce is just a guess","Immediately rewrite the suspected system from scratch, since that always fixes the underlying issue","Close the report, since \"sometimes\" bugs are not real bugs by definition","Ship a fix without reproducing it first, since shipping quickly matters more than confirming the cause"],"w":"The same discipline that applies everywhere else applies here too: a change that \"should\" fix an unreproduced bug is a hope, not a verified fix."},
]
